\DOC LOCAL_MONTSQR_SM2_TAC

\TYPE {LOCAL_MONTSQR_SM2_TAC : int -> string list -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Advances an {eventually}-style x86 machine-state goal across one inlined
Montgomery-squaring subroutine call, replacing the current symbolic state by a
successor state and recording the squaring's specification as a new assumption.

\DESCRIBE
The first argument is an {int} offset/index used to locate the code block to be
executed; the second is a {string list} naming the output and input variables
of the squaring step (the result name and the operand name), which fix the
names appearing in the newly introduced assumptions. The {eventually}
postcondition is unchanged, but its state argument becomes a fresh successor
state variable. Every assumption mentioning the old state — program counter,
loaded bytes, register and memory reads, and the accumulated {MAYCHANGE}
relation from the initial state — is discharged and re-added as the
corresponding fact about the new state, with the {MAYCHANGE} component possibly
extended by one further memory region. The old value stored in the overwritten
destination region is dropped and replaced by a read assumption giving it the
new result variable, and one new conditional arithmetic assumption is added
saying that, under a bound hypothesis on the operand, the result equals the
Montgomery square of the operand modulo the prime. Writing {A'} for this
re-stated and extended assumption list:
{
   A ?- eventually x86 P s
   ========================  LOCAL_MONTSQR_SM2_TAC n ["result"; "operand"]
   A' ?- eventually x86 P s'
}

\COMMENTS
All observed traces show this single kind of step, on goals of this
machine-state form carrying the assumption set of an in-progress symbolic
execution.

\ENDDOC
